The [module tree](/v2/architecture/module-tree) defines your audio architecture. The UI model defines what your user sees and interacts with. Every HISE plugin has a user interface built with the **Content API** — a set of factory methods that create components like knobs, buttons, panels, and combo boxes. These components connect to the module tree through callbacks and property bindings, forming the bridge between user interaction and audio behavior.

Understanding the UI model means understanding three things: how components are created and configured, how they connect to the audio engine, and how they're styled. This page covers all three — the structural layer. For hands-on painting, drawing commands, and advanced styling techniques, see the [Custom UI Design](/v2/guide/custom-ui) guide.


## The Content API

Every HISE interface starts with a single call:

```javascript
Content.makeFrontInterface(900, 600);
```

This designates the current script processor's Content as the main plugin window and sets its size. It must be the first Content call in your interface script's `onInit` callback.

From there, you build the interface by calling factory methods — each one creates a component and returns a reference:

```javascript
const var Cutoff = Content.addKnob("Cutoff", 10, 10);
const var Bypass = Content.addButton("Bypass", 150, 10);
const var Mode   = Content.addComboBox("Mode", 290, 10);
const var Panel1 = Content.addPanel("Panel1", 10, 80);
```

Every factory method takes a unique name and an x/y position. The name is the component's identity — you'll use it to retrieve references, bind properties, and persist preset values. To access a component created elsewhere (or in the Interface Designer), use `Content.getComponent()`:

```javascript
const var Cutoff = Content.getComponent("Cutoff");
```

Always store references as `const var`. The component itself is mutable — you can change its properties, value, and appearance — but the reference binding should never change.

### Static Component Lifetime

This is the most important constraint in HISE's UI model: **all components must be created during `onInit`.** After initialization, the component tree is frozen. You cannot create new sliders, remove existing buttons, or change the number of components at runtime.

The constraint exists because the engine pre-allocates the entire component tree at compile time. Component positions in the tree map directly to preset slots and DAW automation indices. Adding or removing components at runtime would invalidate that mapping, breaking preset recall and automation.

For most plugin UIs this is perfectly fine — a synthesizer's controls don't change while you play. When you do need dynamic UI behavior (scrollable lists, variable-length displays), HISE provides workarounds: `ScriptPanel.addChildPanel()` for creating child panels dynamically, `ScriptedViewport` table mode for data-driven lists, and component preallocation with visibility toggling for switchable layouts. These are covered in [ScriptPanel](#scriptpanel-the-custom-component) below.

### Factory Methods

| Method | Returns | Primary Use |
|--------|---------|-------------|
| `Content.addKnob()` | `ScriptSlider` | Rotary knobs and linear sliders |
| `Content.addButton()` | `ScriptButton` | Toggle and momentary buttons |
| `Content.addComboBox()` | `ScriptComboBox` | Dropdown selectors |
| `Content.addPanel()` | `ScriptPanel` | Custom drawing, containers, interactive controls |
| `Content.addLabel()` | `ScriptLabel` | Text display and input |
| `Content.addTable()` | `ScriptTable` | Curve editors (velocity, envelopes) |
| `Content.addSliderPack()` | `ScriptSliderPack` | Multi-slider arrays |
| `Content.addAudioWaveform()` | `ScriptAudioWaveform` | Sample and buffer display |
| `Content.addImage()` | `ScriptImage` | Static and filmstrip images |
| `Content.addViewport()` | `ScriptedViewport` | Scrollable lists and tables |
| `Content.addFloatingTile()` | `ScriptFloatingTile` | Embeds built-in HISE widgets |
| `Content.addWebView()` | `ScriptWebView` | Embedded web content |


## Component Types

The six core component types cover the vast majority of plugin interfaces:

| Type | What it does | Modes / Variants |
|------|-------------|------------------|
| **ScriptSlider** | Continuous parameter control | Knob, Horizontal, Vertical, Range |
| **ScriptButton** | Binary on/off control | Toggle, momentary, radio groups |
| **ScriptComboBox** | Selection from a list | Dropdown with string items |
| **ScriptPanel** | Anything custom | Paint routines, mouse input, timers, child panels |
| **ScriptLabel** | Text display or input | Static text, editable text fields |
| **ScriptTable** | Curve editing | Velocity curves, waveshaping, envelope shapes |

`ScriptSlider` is named "Knob" in the factory method but covers all continuous controls — rotary knobs, horizontal sliders, vertical faders, and range sliders, selected via the `mode` property.

`ScriptPanel` is the escape hatch. When no standard component fits — a peak meter, an XY pad, a sequencer grid, a custom waveform display — you build it as a panel with a paint routine and mouse callbacks. It's the most architecturally important component type and gets its own section below.

### Specialized Types

Beyond the core six, HISE provides specialized components for specific data types:

- **ScriptSliderPack** — an array of sliders for multi-value editing (step sequencers, harmonic editors)
- **ScriptAudioWaveform** — waveform display bound to audio buffers or sample data
- **ScriptedViewport** — scrollable container with an optional table mode for dynamic, data-driven lists
- **ScriptFloatingTile** — embeds pre-built HISE widgets (preset browsers, keyboards, analysers)
- **ScriptWebView** — embedded web browser for HTML/JS-based UI elements

### Automatability

Only four component types can be exposed as DAW automation parameters: **ScriptSlider**, **ScriptButton**, **ScriptComboBox**, and **ScriptPanel**. Labels, tables, waveforms, and other display-oriented types cannot be automated — they're for visual feedback or data editing, not single-value parameter control.


## Component Properties and the Interface Designer

Every component has a set of properties that control its position, appearance, behavior, and connections. Properties can be set in script or through the **Interface Designer** — HISE's visual layout editor.

### The Property System

Read and write properties with `get()` and `set()`:

```javascript
Cutoff.set("text", "Filter Cutoff");
Cutoff.set("min", 20);
Cutoff.set("max", 20000);
Cutoff.set("middlePosition", 1000);       // skew point for logarithmic scaling
Cutoff.set("defaultValue", 1000);
Cutoff.set("suffix", " Hz");

var currentMin = Cutoff.get("min");        // 20
```

Properties fall into four categories:

| Category | Key Properties | Purpose |
|----------|---------------|---------|
| **Position** | `x`, `y`, `width`, `height`, `parentComponent` | Where the component sits |
| **Appearance** | `text`, `bgColour`, `itemColour`, `itemColour2`, `textColour` | How it looks |
| **Behavior** | `saveInPreset`, `isPluginParameter`, `pluginParameterName`, `enabled`, `visible` | What it does in the system |
| **Connections** | `processorId`, `parameterId`, `macroControl` | What it controls |

The four-slot color system (`bgColour`, `itemColour`, `itemColour2`, `textColour`) is shared by all component types. Each type interprets the slots differently — `bgColour` is a knob's background but a button's off-state color. The Look and Feel system overrides this entirely, so these colors matter most during prototyping.

### Zero-Code Parameter Binding

The `processorId` and `parameterId` properties connect a component directly to a module tree parameter — no callback code required:

```javascript
Cutoff.set("processorId", "PolyphonicFilter1");
Cutoff.set("parameterId", "Frequency");
```

With these two properties set, moving the knob changes the filter's frequency, and automation or preset recall updates the knob's position. The binding is bidirectional and automatic. This is the fastest way to wire your UI to the audio engine and covers the majority of simple parameter connections.

For anything more complex — controlling multiple parameters from one component, applying curves or scaling, triggering side effects — use a control callback instead:

```javascript
inline function onCutoffControl(component, value)
{
    PolyphonicFilter1.setAttribute(PolyphonicFilter1.Frequency, value);
    // additional logic here
}

Cutoff.setControlCallback(onCutoffControl);
```

### The Interface Designer

The Interface Designer is the visual counterpart to script-based UI building. It provides drag-and-drop placement, resize handles, and a property panel for editing any component's properties without writing code.

The designer and your script share the same component data. Components created in script appear in the designer; properties edited in the designer are accessible from script. This round-trip workflow lets you rough out layouts visually and refine behavior in code.

### Parent-Child Hierarchy

HISE uses a flat component list with logical hierarchy established through the `parentComponent` property. When a component specifies a parent:

- Its `x`/`y` coordinates become relative to the parent's top-left corner
- It clips to the parent's bounds
- Showing or hiding the parent affects all children

This enables the most common multi-page UI pattern — use panels as page containers and toggle their visibility:

```javascript
const var SynthPage = Content.addPanel("SynthPage", 0, 50);
const var FXPage    = Content.addPanel("FXPage", 0, 50);
// child components set parentComponent = "SynthPage" or "FXPage"

inline function showPage(index)
{
    SynthPage.showControl(index == 0);
    FXPage.showControl(index == 1);
}
```

This pattern scales well — real-world HISE projects routinely use it with 4+ pages and dozens of child components per page.


## ScriptPanel: The Custom Component

ScriptPanel is the only component type with custom drawing, mouse interaction, timer callbacks, and child panel support. It serves three distinct roles depending on how you use it:

**As a custom control** — paint routines and mouse callbacks let you build any control that doesn't map to a standard type: XY pads, sequencer grids, waveform displays, custom sliders with non-standard behavior.

**As a layout container** — panels with no scripted behavior act as visibility-toggled containers for grouping child components into pages, sections, or overlay layers. This is the most common use of ScriptPanel by sheer count.

**As a real-time display** — timer callbacks poll audio engine state (peak levels, modulator output, playback position) and trigger repaints, creating live meters, modulation indicators, and animated visualizers.

### Paint, Mouse, Timer

Three callbacks define a panel's behavior:

```javascript
const var Meter = Content.addPanel("Meter", 10, 10);
Meter.set("width", 200);
Meter.set("height", 30);

// Visual appearance
Meter.setPaintRoutine(function(g)
{
    g.fillAll(0xFF222222);
    g.setColour(0xFF3399FF);
    g.fillRect([0, 0, this.data.level * this.getWidth(), this.getHeight()]);
});

// Periodic polling (30ms = ~33fps)
Meter.setTimerCallback(function()
{
    this.data.level = Engine.getMasterPeakLevel(0);  // left channel
    this.repaint();
});

Meter.startTimer(30);
```

The paint routine receives a graphics object (`g`) with a full set of drawing commands — rectangles, ellipses, paths, text, gradients, images, and more. Inside paint routines, `this` refers to the panel, so the same routine can be reused across multiple panels of different sizes.

Mouse callbacks turn panels into interactive controls:

```javascript
Panel1.setMouseCallback(function(event)
{
    if (event.clicked)
        Console.print("Clicked at " + event.x + ", " + event.y);

    if (event.hover)
        this.repaint();
});
```

Before a panel responds to mouse events, set its `allowCallbacks` property — `"Clicks Only"`, `"Clicks & Hover"`, `"Clicks, Hover & Dragging"`, or `"All Callbacks"` — to control which events fire.

For the complete drawing API, rectangle layout methods, color system, and interactive control patterns, see [Custom UI Design](/v2/guide/custom-ui).

### Dynamic UI with Child Panels

While the Content API enforces static component lifetime, `ScriptPanel.addChildPanel()` provides a runtime escape hatch. Child panels are full-featured ScriptPanels — they support paint routines, mouse callbacks, timers, and further nesting:

```javascript
const var Container = Content.addPanel("Container", 0, 0);

// At runtime — not limited to onInit
var child = Container.addChildPanel();
child.set("width", 200);
child.set("height", 40);
child.setPaintRoutine(function(g)
{
    g.fillAll(0xFF444444);
});
```

Only panels can be created this way — no dynamic sliders, buttons, or combo boxes. For dynamic lists with mixed component types, `ScriptedViewport` in table mode provides a data-driven row/column system with built-in scroll handling and row recycling. For switchable parameter layouts (like an FX rack where each effect has different controls), the preallocation pattern works well: create the maximum number of controls in `onInit`, then show/hide and reassign `processorId` bindings at runtime.


## Look and Feel

Every standard component type — sliders, buttons, combo boxes, and more — has a default visual appearance. The **Look and Feel (LAF)** system lets you override how any component draws without replacing it with a ScriptPanel.

Create a LAF object, register draw functions for the component types you want to customize, and attach it:

```javascript
const var laf = Content.createLocalLookAndFeel();

laf.registerFunction("drawRotarySlider", function(g, obj)
{
    var area = obj.area;
    var angle = obj.valueNormalized * Math.PI * 1.5 - Math.PI * 0.75;

    // Background arc
    g.setColour(0xFF333333);
    g.drawArc(area.reduced(4), -Math.PI * 0.75, Math.PI * 0.75, 3.0);

    // Value arc
    g.setColour(0xFF3399FF);
    g.drawArc(area.reduced(4), -Math.PI * 0.75, angle, 3.0);
});

Cutoff.setLocalLookAndFeel(laf);
```

Each registered function receives the graphics object and an `obj` parameter containing the component's current state — area, value, hover state, text, and more. Over 60 draw functions are registerable, covering sliders, buttons, combo boxes, preset browsers, table editors, AHDSR graphs, MIDI keyboards, filter displays, analysers, and meters.

### Global vs Local Scope

A **local LAF** (`Content.createLocalLookAndFeel()`) applies only to the components you explicitly attach it to. A **global LAF** (`Engine.createGlobalScriptLookAndFeel()`) applies to all matching component types across the entire plugin. Use local LAFs when different sections need different styles; use a global LAF when you want a unified look with minimal wiring.

### Three Styling Approaches

| Approach | Best For | Mechanism |
|----------|----------|-----------|
| **Scripted LAF** | Continuous, data-driven rendering | JavaScript draw functions via `registerFunction()` |
| **CSS Stylesheets** | State-based components | `laf.setInlineStyleSheet()` or `.css` files |
| **Filmstrip Images** | Pre-rendered, pixel-perfect controls | PNG image strips via component `filmstrip` property |

The choice between scripted and CSS comes down to what drives the visual: **CSS excels at discrete states** — hover, active, checked, disabled — using familiar pseudo-selectors and transitions. **Scripted LAF excels at continuous data** — arc angles computed from a knob's value, gradient fills driven by modulation depth, real-time positions. Rotary knobs are the canonical example: they require `Path.addArc()` with angles derived from the knob's normalized value, which is fundamentally procedural geometry that CSS cannot express. HISE also supports a **CombinedLaf** that dispatches per-function — CSS renders the buttons and combo boxes, scripted code renders the knobs and visualizers.

For complete LAF coverage — all 60 draw functions, CSS syntax, the `obj` parameter for each function, and the CombinedLaf pattern — see [Custom UI Design](/v2/guide/custom-ui).


## Floating Tiles

Floating Tiles are **pre-built, self-contained UI widgets** that you embed into your interface via `Content.addFloatingTile()`. They provide complex features — preset browsing, MIDI keyboards, audio analysers — without custom drawing code.

```javascript
const var PresetTile = Content.addFloatingTile("PresetTile", 0, 0);
PresetTile.set("width", 900);
PresetTile.set("height", 500);

PresetTile.setContentData({
    "Type": "PresetBrowser",
    "Font": "Oxygen Bold",
    "FontSize": 18,
    "ShowFolderButton": false
});
```

Each tile type accepts a JSON configuration object. You set the type, configure its properties, and the tile handles all internal behavior — user interaction, data management, and rendering.

Key Floating Tile types:

| Category | Types |
|----------|-------|
| **Presets & Settings** | `PresetBrowser`, `CustomSettings`, `MidiLearnPanel` |
| **Keyboard & MIDI** | `Keyboard`, `MidiSources`, `MidiChannelList`, `MPEPanel` |
| **Visualizers** | `AudioAnalyser`, `FilterGraph`, `DraggableFilterPanel`, `Plotter` |
| **Envelopes** | `AHDSRGraph`, `FlexAHDSRGraph` |
| **Meters & Status** | `MatrixPeakMeter`, `PerformanceLabel`, `ActivityLed` |
| **Content** | `MarkdownPanel`, `TooltipPanel`, `AboutPagePanel` |
| **Modulation** | `ModulationMatrix`, `ModulationMatrixController` |

`PresetBrowser` is the most commonly used — it provides a three-column browser (bank / category / preset) with search, favorites, and save/delete functionality out of the box. Floating Tiles can be styled with the Look and Feel system — functions like `drawPresetBrowserListItem` and `drawWhiteNote` let you customize their appearance to match your plugin's design.


## What's Next

- **Next →** [Data Model & User Presets](/v2/architecture/data-model) — how component values persist through presets and DAW sessions
- **Deep dive →** [Custom UI Design](/v2/guide/custom-ui) — Look and Feel, ScriptPanel painting, CSS, and visual feedback in depth
- **Build one →** [Building a Sampler](/v2/guide/building-a-sampler) — apply the UI model to a real project

