Component Properties and the Interface Designer

Every component has a set of properties that control its position, appearance, behavior, and connections. Properties can be set in script or through the **Interface Designer** — HISE's visual layout editor.

### The Property System

Read and write properties with `get()` and `set()`:

```javascript
Cutoff.set("text", "Filter Cutoff");
Cutoff.set("min", 20);
Cutoff.set("max", 20000);
Cutoff.set("middlePosition", 1000);       // skew point for logarithmic scaling
Cutoff.set("defaultValue", 1000);
Cutoff.set("suffix", " Hz");

var currentMin = Cutoff.get("min");        // 20
```

Properties fall into four categories:

| Category | Key Properties | Purpose |
|----------|---------------|---------|
| **Position** | `x`, `y`, `width`, `height`, `parentComponent` | Where the component sits |
| **Appearance** | `text`, `bgColour`, `itemColour`, `itemColour2`, `textColour` | How it looks |
| **Behavior** | `saveInPreset`, `isPluginParameter`, `pluginParameterName`, `enabled`, `visible` | What it does in the system |
| **Connections** | `processorId`, `parameterId`, `macroControl` | What it controls |

The four-slot color system (`bgColour`, `itemColour`, `itemColour2`, `textColour`) is shared by all component types. Each type interprets the slots differently — `bgColour` is a knob's background but a button's off-state color. The Look and Feel system overrides this entirely, so these colors matter most during prototyping.

### Zero-Code Parameter Binding

The `processorId` and `parameterId` properties connect a component directly to a module tree parameter — no callback code required:

```javascript
Cutoff.set("processorId", "PolyphonicFilter1");
Cutoff.set("parameterId", "Frequency");
```

With these two properties set, moving the knob changes the filter's frequency, and automation or preset recall updates the knob's position. The binding is bidirectional and automatic. This is the fastest way to wire your UI to the audio engine and covers the majority of simple parameter connections.

For anything more complex — controlling multiple parameters from one component, applying curves or scaling, triggering side effects — use a control callback instead:

```javascript
inline function onCutoffControl(component, value)
{
    PolyphonicFilter1.setAttribute(PolyphonicFilter1.Frequency, value);
    // additional logic here
}

Cutoff.setControlCallback(onCutoffControl);
```

### The Interface Designer

The Interface Designer is the visual counterpart to script-based UI building. It provides drag-and-drop placement, resize handles, and a property panel for editing any component's properties without writing code.

The designer and your script share the same component data. Components created in script appear in the designer; properties edited in the designer are accessible from script. This round-trip workflow lets you rough out layouts visually and refine behavior in code.

### Parent-Child Hierarchy

HISE uses a flat component list with logical hierarchy established through the `parentComponent` property. When a component specifies a parent:

- Its `x`/`y` coordinates become relative to the parent's top-left corner
- It clips to the parent's bounds
- Showing or hiding the parent affects all children

This enables the most common multi-page UI pattern — use panels as page containers and toggle their visibility:

```javascript
const var SynthPage = Content.addPanel("SynthPage", 0, 50);
const var FXPage    = Content.addPanel("FXPage", 0, 50);
// child components set parentComponent = "SynthPage" or "FXPage"

inline function showPage(index)
{
    SynthPage.showControl(index == 0);
    FXPage.showControl(index == 1);
}
```

This pattern scales well — real-world HISE projects routinely use it with 4+ pages and dozens of child components per page.

