HiseScript is the control layer of HISE. It doesn't produce audio — the [module tree](/v2/architecture/module-tree) and [signal flow](/v2/architecture/audio-signal-path) handle that. Instead, scripts build the [UI](/v2/architecture/ui-model), process MIDI events, connect knobs to parameters, manage [presets](/v2/architecture/data-model), and orchestrate everything the module tree can't express declaratively.

HiseScript looks like JavaScript — curly braces, familiar operators, `function` keyword — but it's a purpose-built language with stricter variable semantics, no closures, no prototype chain, and a compilation model that JavaScript developers won't expect.


## The Interface Script Pattern

Every HiseScript project follows the same structural pattern in its main `Interface.js`. This is the universal shape — from minimal effects to complex instruments:

```javascript
// ─── 1. Frontend initialization ───
Content.makeFrontInterface(900, 600);

// ─── 2. Includes ───
include("LookAndFeel.js");
include("PageLogic.js");

// ─── 3. Module references ───
const var filter = Synth.getEffect("PolyphonicFilter");
const var sampler = Synth.getSampler("Sustain");
const var reverb = Synth.getEffect("SimpleReverb");

// ─── 4. UI component references ───
const var knobCutoff = Content.getComponent("knobCutoff");
const var knobReverb = Content.getComponent("knobReverb");

// ─── 5. Control callbacks ───
inline function onKnobCutoffControl(component, value)
{
    filter.setAttribute(filter.Frequency, value);
};
knobCutoff.setControlCallback(onKnobCutoffControl);

// ─── 6. MIDI callbacks (empty if not needed) ───
function onNoteOn() {}
function onNoteOff() {}
function onController() {}
function onTimer() {}

// ─── 7. Legacy callback — always empty ───
function onControl(number, value) {}
```

Key elements:

- **`Content.makeFrontInterface()`** always comes first — it sets the plugin window size and initializes the UI system.
- **`include()` paths are relative to the `Scripts/` directory.** Write `include("LookAndFeel.js")`, not `include("Scripts/LookAndFeel.js")`.
- **Module references are always `const var`.** The reference is established once and safely read from multiple threads.
- **`setControlCallback`** is the modern callback mechanism. The legacy `onControl(number, value)` still exists — leave it as an empty function body.
- **All five callback slots must be present**, even if empty.

Everything in this script runs in `onInit` — the initialization callback. After compilation, only registered callbacks fire in response to events.


## Compilation, Not Interpretation

HiseScript doesn't run continuously. It **compiles**. When you press F5 (or HISE starts up), the engine compiles the code, executes `onInit` once, and waits. After that, only **callbacks** fire — in response to MIDI events, UI interactions, or timers.

![Callback flow from compilation through runtime](/images/v2/architecture/scripting-model-callback-flow.svg)

This means **`onInit` is where you build everything.** Module references, UI components, include files, variable declarations — all of it runs at compile time. By the time a MIDI note arrives or a knob turns, the script's structure is already in place. There is no "startup phase" at runtime; `onInit` *is* the startup phase.


## The Five Callback Slots

Every `ScriptProcessor` has exactly five callbacks. The engine calls them automatically:

| Callback | Fires when | Thread |
|----------|-----------|--------|
| `onInit` | Script compiles | — |
| `onNoteOn` | MIDI note-on arrives | Audio (or scripting if deferred) |
| `onNoteOff` | MIDI note-off arrives | Audio (or scripting if deferred) |
| `onController` | MIDI CC arrives | Audio (or scripting if deferred) |
| `onTimer` | Timer interval elapses | Audio (or scripting if deferred) |

MIDI callbacks access the **Message object** — a global API that reads and modifies the current MIDI event:

```javascript
function onNoteOn()
{
    local note = Message.getNoteNumber();
    local vel = Message.getVelocity();

    if (note < 36)
        Message.ignoreEvent(true);  // block this note
}

function onController()
{
    if (Message.getControllerNumber() == 64)
        reg sustainDown = Message.getControllerValue() >= 64;
}
```

`Message.ignoreEvent(true)` blocks a MIDI event from reaching downstream processors. This is how articulation switching works — a keyswitch note enters the root `MidiProcessorChain`, the script detects it, ignores the event, and toggles `MidiMuter` processors to route subsequent notes to the correct [sampler](/v2/architecture/sampling-engine).


## Connecting Script to the Module Tree

This is the bridge between code and audio. Scripts control the module tree through **reference objects** — handles to specific processors obtained once in `onInit`.

| API Call | Returns | Use For |
|----------|---------|---------|
| `Synth.getEffect("name")` | Effect reference | Filter, reverb, EQ, gain control |
| `Synth.getSampler("name")` | Sampler reference | Sample map loading, purge control |
| `Synth.getModulator("name")` | Modulator reference | LFO rate, envelope shape |
| `Synth.getMidiProcessor("name")` | MIDI processor reference | Bypass, attribute control |
| `Synth.getChildSynth("name")` | Sound generator reference | Bypass, voice control |

The string argument matches the **ID** in the Module Tree Editor — the name shown on each processor and in the XML preset.

### setAttribute: How Script Becomes Sound

Once you have a reference, `setAttribute` changes any parameter:

```javascript
const var filter = Synth.getEffect("PolyphonicFilter");
const var reverb = Synth.getEffect("SimpleReverb");
const var vibrato = Synth.getModulator("VibratoLFO");

// Each module type exposes named parameter constants
filter.setAttribute(filter.Frequency, 2000);
filter.setAttribute(filter.Q, 0.7);
reverb.setAttribute(reverb.Mix, 0.35);
vibrato.setAttribute(vibrato.Frequency, 4.5);
```

The parameter constants (`filter.Frequency`, `reverb.Mix`) are auto-generated from the module type. The change takes effect on the next audio buffer.

**This is the core mechanism:** knob turns → callback fires → `setAttribute` updates a module parameter → the audio engine hears the change.

You can also read back and bypass:

```javascript
local freq = filter.getAttribute(filter.Frequency);
filter.setBypassed(true);   // remove from signal chain
filter.setBypassed(false);  // re-enable
```

Bypassing removes a processor from the signal chain without deleting it. This is how `MidiMuter` switching works — toggling bypass on parallel samplers to switch articulations instantly.


## Connecting Script to the UI

UI components follow the same pattern — obtained once, stored as `const var`:

```javascript
const var knobCutoff = Content.getComponent("knobCutoff");
const var btnPages = [Content.getComponent("btnPage0"),
                      Content.getComponent("btnPage1"),
                      Content.getComponent("btnPage2")];
```

### setControlCallback

Register an inline function that fires when a component's value changes:

```javascript
inline function onKnobCutoffControl(component, value)
{
    filter.setAttribute(filter.Frequency, value);
};
knobCutoff.setControlCallback(onKnobCutoffControl);
```

### Grouped Callbacks

When similar components share logic, use one callback with `indexOf`:

```javascript
inline function onBtnPageControl(component, value)
{
    local idx = btnPages.indexOf(component);
    for (local i = 0; i < btnPages.length; i++)
        btnPages[i].setValue(i == idx);
};

for (b in btnPages)
    b.setControlCallback(onBtnPageControl);
```

This pattern scales from 3 buttons to 12 mixer channels without code duplication.

### The Zero-Code Alternative: processorId

For simple 1:1 bindings, skip scripting entirely. Set a component's `processorId` and `parameterId` properties in the Interface Designer:

- **processorId** → the module's ID (`"PolyphonicFilter"`)
- **parameterId** → the parameter name (`"Frequency"`)

The binding happens automatically. The **Connection Wizard** in the IDE automates this. Use `processorId` for direct bindings; switch to `setControlCallback` when you need logic, arithmetic, or one knob controlling multiple parameters.

For reactive event systems beyond simple callbacks — the Broadcaster pattern for scaling to complex UIs — see [Data Flow & Messaging](/v2/architecture/data-flow-messaging) for the architecture, and [Events & Messaging](/v2/guide/events-and-messaging) for practical patterns.


## Variable Types & Thread Safety

HiseScript has four variable types. The choice determines thread safety and lifetime:

| Type | Lifetime | Thread Safety | Use For |
|------|----------|---------------|---------|
| `const var` | Script lifetime | Safe everywhere | Module references, UI handles, lookup tables |
| `var` | Script lifetime | Scripting thread only | UI state: page index, mode flags |
| `reg` | Script lifetime | Audio-thread safe | MIDI callback state: RR counters, last note |
| `local` | Function scope | Always safe | Loop variables, temporary calculations |

```javascript
const var filter = Synth.getEffect("Filter");   // immutable reference
var currentPage = 0;                             // UI state
reg lastNote = -1;                               // MIDI state (audio thread)

inline function doSomething() {
    local i = 0;                                 // temporary
};
```

**The rule:** `const var` for shared references. `reg` for state that MIDI callbacks touch on the audio thread. `var` for scripting-thread-only state. `local` for temporaries. `inline function` for all reusable logic — helpers, callbacks, utilities.

**Threading note:** MIDI callbacks run on the audio thread by default. `Synth.deferCallbacks(true)` moves them to the scripting thread — losing sample-accurate timing but enabling heavy operations like sample map loading. Most sampler projects defer; synths with tight timing requirements may not.


## Script Organization

As projects grow, script organization scales across three tiers:

**Tier 1 — Single File** (< 200 lines). Everything in `Interface.js`. Works for simple effects and educational projects.

**Tier 2 — Modular Namespaces** (10–35 files). Split by domain, connect via `include()`. Namespaces prevent name collisions:

```javascript
// LookAndFeel.js
namespace LAF
{
    const var main = Content.createLocalLookAndFeel();

    main.registerFunction("drawRotarySlider", function(g, obj)
    {
        // custom knob rendering
    });
};
```

This is the most common pattern in shipping plugins.

**Tier 3 — Data/UI Separation** (50+ files). Strict directory split: `Data/` for state and logic, `UI/` for panels and rendering. Used by large commercial projects with complex state management.

Split when a file exceeds ~300 lines or covers two unrelated domains.


## What's Next

The scripting model is the glue that connects everything — module tree, UI, presets, and MIDI. With this in place, you have the full picture of how HISE projects work.

- **Next →** [Project Lifecycle](/v2/architecture/project-lifecycle) — from development through presets to export and distribution
- **Build one →** [Building a Sampler](/v2/guide/building-a-sampler) — apply the scripting model to a real instrument
- **Build one →** [Building a Synth](/v2/guide/building-a-synth) — scripting a synth with modulation routing
- **Build one →** [Building an Effect](/v2/guide/building-an-effect) — minimal scripting for an FX plugin

