This guide covers the hands-on techniques for building custom plugin interfaces in HISE. It assumes you've read [The UI Model](/v2/architecture/ui-model), which explains the Content API, component types, and structural concepts. Here you'll learn how to actually draw things — paint routines for fully custom controls, Look and Feel for restyling built-in components, CSS for state-driven styling, and timer-driven displays for real-time visual feedback.

Two systems share the same drawing API:

- **ScriptPanel paint routines** — you draw a component from scratch. Full creative freedom; you handle everything.
- **Look and Feel (LAF) functions** — you override how a built-in component type draws. The engine calls your function with the component's current state; you draw based on that state.

Both receive a graphics object (`g`) with the same set of drawing commands. Learn one, and you know the other.


## Paint Routines

Graphics code in HISE uses a **graphics object** (conventionally named `g`) to draw shapes, text, and colors onto a surface. You'll use this object in two contexts: **ScriptPanel paint routines** (custom controls from scratch) and **Look and Feel functions** (customizing built-in components). Both use the same drawing commands.

### Setting Up a Paint Routine

Call `setPaintRoutine()` on a panel reference and pass it a function with one parameter — the graphics object:

```javascript
const var Panel1 = Content.getComponent("Panel1");

Panel1.setPaintRoutine(function(g)
{
    g.fillAll(0xFF333333);
});
```

Once assigned, the paint routine controls the panel's appearance completely. If it draws nothing, the panel is invisible.

### Colors

Colors can be specified as hex integers in `0xAARRGGBB` format (`AA` = alpha, `RR` = red, `GG` = green, `BB` = blue), or using the `Colours` namespace:

```javascript
g.setColour(0xFFFF0000);                                 // opaque red
g.setColour(Colours.white);                              // named constant
g.setColour(Colours.withAlpha(Colours.dodgerblue, 0.5)); // 50% transparent
```

### The Rectangle Object

Most drawing commands take an area parameter. While you can pass a plain `[x, y, width, height]` array, the `Rectangle` object is preferred — it wraps JUCE's `Rectangle<float>` class and provides methods for subdivision and layout:

```javascript
var area = Rectangle(10, 10, 200, 100);

g.fillRect(area);                        // fill the rectangle
g.fillRect(area.reduced(5));             // fill with 5px inset on all sides
g.fillRect(area.translated(50, 0));      // shifted 50px to the right
```

Key layout methods:

| Method | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| `reduced(amount)` | Returns a smaller rectangle, inset on all sides |
| `removeFromTop(amount)` | Removes and returns a strip from the top, shrinking the original |
| `removeFromLeft(amount)` | Removes and returns a strip from the left, shrinking the original |
| `removeFromBottom(amount)` | Removes and returns a strip from the bottom |
| `removeFromRight(amount)` | Removes and returns a strip from the right |
| `translated(dx, dy)` | Returns a shifted rectangle |
| `withHeight(h)` | Returns same position, different height |
| `withSizeKeepingCentre(w, h)` | Returns resized around centre point |

The `removeFrom...` methods are particularly powerful — each call slices off a portion and modifies the original in place. This lets you progressively divide an area into rows or columns without manual coordinate math:

```javascript
Panel1.setPaintRoutine(function(g)
{
    var area = this.getLocalBounds(0);

    // Slice layout: header strip on top, remaining area below
    var header = area.removeFromTop(30);
    var body = area; // area has been shrunk by removeFromTop

    g.setColour(0xFF444444);
    g.fillRect(header);
    g.setColour(0xFF222222);
    g.fillRect(body);
});
```

### The `this` Keyword

Inside a paint routine, `this` refers to the panel. This lets you write routines that adapt to the panel's size:

```javascript
Panel1.setPaintRoutine(function(g)
{
    var area = this.getLocalBounds(0); // [0, 0, width, height]

    g.fillAll(0xFF333333);
    g.setColour(0xFFFFFFFF);
    g.drawRect(area, 2.0);
});
```

Because `this` resolves to whichever panel the routine is attached to, you can assign the same paint function to multiple panels and each draws correctly at its own size.

### Drawing Commands

Every `draw` command renders an outline; every `fill` command renders a solid shape. The active color (set with `g.setColour()`) applies to all subsequent commands until changed. Commands are drawn in order — each shape paints on top of everything before it. Structure your routine **back to front**: background → borders → foreground → text.

| Command | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| `fillAll(colour)` | Fills the entire surface |
| `setColour(colour)` | Sets the active color |
| `fillRect(area)` | Fills a rectangle |
| `drawRect(area, borderSize)` | Draws a rectangle outline |
| `fillRoundedRectangle(area, cornerSize)` | Fills a rounded rectangle |
| `drawRoundedRectangle(area, cornerSize, borderSize)` | Draws a rounded rectangle outline |
| `fillEllipse(area)` | Fills an ellipse (circle if width = height) |
| `drawEllipse(area, borderSize)` | Draws an ellipse outline |
| `drawLine(x1, y1, x2, y2, lineThickness)` | Draws a straight line |
| `drawAlignedText(text, area, alignment)` | Draws text (`"left"`, `"centred"`, `"right"`) |
| `setFont(fontName, fontSize)` | Sets the font for subsequent text |
| `setGradientFill(gradientData)` | Sets a gradient instead of a solid color |
| `drawImage(imageName, area, ox, oy)` | Draws a loaded image |
| `fillPath(path, area)` | Fills a Path object |
| `drawPath(path, area, strokeThickness)` | Draws a Path outline |

For the full list (51 drawing commands including layers, shadows, blur, and SVG), type `Graphics` in the API Browser.

### Paths

Paths let you draw arbitrary shapes — arcs, curves, complex outlines. Create a `Path` object and add segments to it:

```javascript
const var p = Content.createPath();

// Arc for a knob indicator
p.addArc([0, 0, 1, 1], -2.5, 1.3);

// Inside a paint routine:
g.setColour(0xFF3399FF);
g.drawPath(p, area.reduced(6), 3.0);
```

Paths are defined in a normalized `[0, 0, 1, 1]` coordinate space and scaled to the target area when drawn. Key path methods:

| Method | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| `startNewSubPath(x, y)` | Moves to a point without drawing |
| `lineTo(x, y)` | Draws a line to a point |
| `quadraticTo(cx, cy, x, y)` | Draws a quadratic curve |
| `addArc(area, startAngle, endAngle)` | Adds a circular arc (radians) |
| `closeSubPath()` | Closes the current shape |
| `clear()` | Removes all segments |

### Custom Fonts

Load fonts once in `onInit`, then reference by ID:

```javascript
Engine.loadFontAs("{PROJECT_FOLDER}fonts/MyFont.ttf", "MyFont");

// Inside a paint routine:
g.setFont("MyFont", 24);
g.drawAlignedText("Hello", area, "centred");
```

### Triggering a Repaint

A paint routine runs when the panel initializes and when its value changes. To trigger a repaint manually:

```javascript
Panel1.repaint();       // from outside
this.repaint();         // from a callback on the same panel
```


## Building Custom Controls

Paint routines become genuinely useful when combined with **mouse callbacks** and **`this.data`** for state storage. Together, these three pieces let you build fully interactive controls.

### Mouse Callbacks

Panels respond to mouse input through `setMouseCallback()`:

```javascript
Panel1.setMouseCallback(function(event)
{
    if (event.clicked)
        Console.print("Clicked at " + event.x + ", " + event.y);

    if (event.hover)
        this.repaint();
});
```

Before a panel responds to mouse events, set its `allowCallbacks` property to control which events fire:

| Level | Events |
|-------|--------|
| `"No Callbacks"` | None (default) |
| `"Context Menu"` | Right-click popup only |
| `"Clicks Only"` | Click, double-click, mouse-up |
| `"Clicks & Hover"` | + hover enter/exit |
| `"Clicks, Hover & Dragging"` | + drag events |
| `"All Callbacks"` | + every mouse movement |

Key event properties:

| Property | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| `event.clicked` | True on mouse-down |
| `event.mouseUp` | True on mouse-up |
| `event.doubleClick` | True on double-click |
| `event.rightClick` | True if right button |
| `event.hover` | True when hovering (requires `"Clicks & Hover"+`) |
| `event.drag` | True during drag (requires `"Clicks, Hover & Dragging"+`) |
| `event.x`, `event.y` | Current mouse position |
| `event.dragX`, `event.dragY` | Distance dragged from start |
| `event.shiftDown`, `event.cmdDown`, `event.altDown` | Modifier keys |

### Example: A Custom Toggle Button

A minimal toggle that draws a filled circle when on, an outlined circle when off, and highlights on hover:

```javascript
const var Toggle = Content.addPanel("Toggle", 10, 10);
Toggle.set("width", 40);
Toggle.set("height", 40);
Toggle.set("allowCallbacks", "Clicks & Hover");
Toggle.set("saveInPreset", true);

Toggle.setPaintRoutine(function(g)
{
    var a = this.getLocalBounds(0).reduced(4);
    var on = this.getValue();
    var hov = this.data.hover;

    // Background
    g.setColour(hov ? 0xFF444444 : 0xFF333333);
    g.fillEllipse(a);

    if (on)
    {
        g.setColour(0xFF3399FF);
        g.fillEllipse(a.reduced(6));
    }
    else
    {
        g.setColour(0xFF888888);
        g.drawEllipse(a.reduced(6), 2.0);
    }
});

Toggle.setMouseCallback(function(event)
{
    if (event.clicked)
    {
        this.setValue(1 - this.getValue());
        this.changed();   // triggers control callback + preset save
    }

    this.data.hover = event.hover;
    this.repaint();
});
```

The key pattern: `this.setValue()` updates the stored value, and `this.changed()` tells the engine the value changed — triggering any attached control callback and marking the preset as dirty. Without `this.changed()`, the value updates visually but the engine doesn't know about it.

### Example: A Custom Slider

A horizontal slider using drag events. The drag distance maps to the value range:

```javascript
const var Slider1 = Content.addPanel("Slider1", 10, 60);
Slider1.set("width", 200);
Slider1.set("height", 30);
Slider1.set("allowCallbacks", "Clicks, Hover & Dragging");
Slider1.set("saveInPreset", true);

Slider1.setPaintRoutine(function(g)
{
    var a = this.getLocalBounds(0);
    var val = this.getValue();

    // Track background
    g.setColour(0xFF333333);
    g.fillRoundedRectangle(a.reduced(0, 8), 4);

    // Filled portion
    g.setColour(0xFF3399FF);
    var filled = a.reduced(0, 8);
    filled = filled.withWidth(filled.getWidth() * val);
    g.fillRoundedRectangle(filled, 4);

    // Value text
    g.setColour(0xFFFFFFFF);
    g.drawAlignedText(Math.round(val * 100) + "%", a, "centred");
});

Slider1.setMouseCallback(function(event)
{
    if (event.clicked)
    {
        // Store the starting value for drag reference
        this.data.startValue = this.getValue();
    }

    if (event.drag)
    {
        // Map horizontal drag to 0–1 range
        var delta = event.dragX / this.getWidth();
        var newVal = Math.range(this.data.startValue + delta, 0, 1);
        this.setValue(newVal);
        this.changed();
        this.repaint();
    }

    if (event.doubleClick)
    {
        this.setValue(0.5);   // reset to default
        this.changed();
        this.repaint();
    }
});
```

This demonstrates the drag pattern: capture the starting value on `event.clicked`, compute a delta on `event.drag`, and clamp the result. The `event.dragX` property gives cumulative horizontal distance from the click point.

### Using `this.data`

Every ScriptPanel has a `this.data` object — a free-form property bag for storing custom state. It persists across paint/mouse/timer callbacks but is **not saved in presets**. Use it for transient UI state: hover flags, animation progress, cached calculations, drag start positions.

```javascript
this.data.hover = event.hover;           // hover state for paint routine
this.data.startValue = this.getValue();  // drag reference
this.data.level = 0.0;                   // meter level from timer
this.data.animationPhase = 0.0;          // animation counter
```

For persisted state, use `this.getValue()` / `this.setValue()` — these participate in the preset and automation system.


## Look and Feel

Look and Feel (LAF) lets you override how **built-in component types** draw without replacing them with ScriptPanels. The components retain all their built-in behavior — value handling, automation, preset saving, mouse interaction — you just replace the visuals.

### Creating and Attaching a LAF

```javascript
const var laf = Content.createLocalLookAndFeel();

laf.registerFunction("drawRotarySlider", function(g, obj)
{
    // Your custom drawing code here
});

// Attach to specific components
myKnob1.setLocalLookAndFeel(laf);
myKnob2.setLocalLookAndFeel(laf);
```

One LAF object can serve multiple components. Register draw functions for the component types you want to customize — everything else renders with the default appearance.

### The `obj` Parameter

Every LAF function receives `function(g, obj)` where `obj` contains the component's current state. The properties vary by function, but the core set is consistent:

**Common to all LAF functions:**

| Property | Type | Description |
|----------|------|-------------|
| `obj.area` | Rectangle | The component's drawable area |
| `obj.text` | String | The component's display text |
| `obj.enabled` | bool | Whether the component is enabled |
| `obj.bgColour` | int | Background color (from property) |
| `obj.itemColour1` | int | First accent color |
| `obj.itemColour2` | int | Second accent color |
| `obj.textColour` | int | Text color |

**`drawRotarySlider` adds:**

| Property | Type | Description |
|----------|------|-------------|
| `obj.value` | double | Current value (in the component's range) |
| `obj.valueNormalized` | double | Value mapped to 0.0–1.0 |
| `obj.valueSuffixString` | String | Formatted value with suffix (e.g. `"440.0 Hz"`) |
| `obj.suffix` | String | Just the suffix (e.g. `" Hz"`) |
| `obj.min`, `obj.max` | double | Value range |
| `obj.skew` | double | Skew factor |
| `obj.clicked` | bool | Mouse button is down |
| `obj.hover` | bool | Mouse is over the component |
| `obj.modulationActive` | bool | A modulation source is connected |
| `obj.valueFromModulation` | double | Modulated normalized value (0.0–1.0) |
| `obj.modMinValue`, `obj.modMaxValue` | double | Modulation range limits (normalized) |

**`drawToggleButton` adds:**

| Property | Type | Description |
|----------|------|-------------|
| `obj.value` | bool | Current toggle state |
| `obj.over` | bool | Mouse is hovering |
| `obj.down` | bool | Mouse button is down |

**`drawComboBox` adds:**

| Property | Type | Description |
|----------|------|-------------|
| `obj.text` | String | Currently selected item text |
| `obj.active` | bool | An item is selected |
| `obj.hover` | bool | Mouse is over or popup is open |
| `obj.down` | bool | Mouse button is down |

### Drawing a Rotary Slider

The most common LAF function. Rotary knobs need arc geometry — you compute start and end angles from the normalized value, then draw the arc:

```javascript
laf.registerFunction("drawRotarySlider", function(g, obj)
{
    var a = obj.area;
    var val = obj.valueNormalized;

    // Angle range: -135° to +135° (in radians)
    var startAngle = -2.356;   // -3π/4
    var endAngle = 2.356;      //  3π/4
    var currentAngle = startAngle + val * (endAngle - startAngle);

    // Background track
    g.setColour(0xFF333333);
    g.drawArc(a.reduced(6), startAngle, endAngle, 3.0);

    // Value arc
    g.setColour(0xFF3399FF);
    g.drawArc(a.reduced(6), startAngle, currentAngle, 3.0);

    // Modulation ring (when a modulator is connected)
    if (obj.modulationActive)
    {
        var modAngle = startAngle + obj.valueFromModulation * (endAngle - startAngle);
        g.setColour(0x5500FF88);
        g.drawArc(a.reduced(2), currentAngle, modAngle, 2.0);
    }

    // Value text
    g.setColour(obj.hover ? 0xFFFFFFFF : 0xFFAAAAAA);
    g.setFont("default", 12.0);
    g.drawAlignedText(obj.hover ? obj.valueSuffixString : obj.text,
                      a, "centred");
});
```

The modulation ring is a key detail for synth UIs — when a modulator is routed to a knob, `obj.modulationActive` becomes true and `obj.valueFromModulation` reflects the modulated position. Drawing a second arc between the base value and the modulated value gives the user visual feedback on modulation depth.

### Drawing a Toggle Button

Buttons are simpler — they have discrete states rather than continuous values:

```javascript
laf.registerFunction("drawToggleButton", function(g, obj)
{
    var a = obj.area;

    // Background
    g.setColour(obj.value ? 0xFF3399FF : 0xFF333333);
    g.fillRoundedRectangle(a, 4.0);

    // Hover highlight
    if (obj.over)
    {
        g.setColour(0x15FFFFFF);
        g.fillRoundedRectangle(a, 4.0);
    }

    // Label
    g.setColour(obj.value ? 0xFFFFFFFF : 0xFF999999);
    g.setFont("default", 13.0);
    g.drawAlignedText(obj.text, a, "centred");
});
```

`drawToggleButton` is the most frequently customized LAF function — buttons vary the most across plugin designs (icon buttons, pill toggles, tab selectors, solo/mute buttons).

### Drawing a Combo Box

When you register `drawComboBox`, you take full control of the dropdown's closed-state appearance. HISE hides the default text label so you can draw everything yourself:

```javascript
laf.registerFunction("drawComboBox", function(g, obj)
{
    var a = obj.area;

    // Background
    g.setColour(obj.hover ? 0xFF444444 : 0xFF333333);
    g.fillRoundedRectangle(a, 4.0);

    // Selected item text
    g.setColour(0xFFFFFFFF);
    g.setFont("default", 14.0);
    g.drawAlignedText(obj.text, [a[0] + 10, a[1], a[2] - 30, a[3]], "left");

    // Dropdown arrow
    var arrow = Content.createPath();
    arrow.startNewSubPath(0.0, 0.0);
    arrow.lineTo(0.5, 1.0);
    arrow.lineTo(1.0, 0.0);

    g.setColour(0xFFAAAAAA);
    g.fillPath(arrow, [a[0] + a[2] - 22, a[1] + a[3] * 0.5 - 3, 12, 6]);
});
```

### Other Key Draw Functions

Beyond the three core types, LAF covers the entire HISE widget set. Some commonly used functions:

| Function | Component | Key `obj` Properties |
|----------|-----------|---------------------|
| `drawLinearSlider` | Horizontal/vertical sliders | Same as rotary, plus `obj.style` |
| `drawPopupMenuBackground` | Popup menu container | `obj.area` |
| `drawPopupMenuItem` | Popup menu item | `obj.text`, `obj.ticked`, `obj.highlighted`, `obj.isSeparator`, `obj.isSectionHeader` |
| `drawPresetBrowserBackground` | Preset browser | `obj.area` |
| `drawPresetBrowserListItem` | Preset browser item | `obj.text`, `obj.selected`, `obj.area` |
| `drawAhdsrPath` | AHDSR envelope display | `obj.path` (the envelope path), `obj.area` |
| `drawFilterBackground` | Filter frequency display | `obj.area` |
| `drawFilterPath` | Filter response curve | `obj.path`, `obj.area`, `obj.filterIndex` |
| `drawWhiteNote` / `drawBlackNote` | MIDI keyboard | `obj.keyWidth`, `obj.noteNumber`, `obj.on` |
| `drawTablePath` | Table editor curve | `obj.path`, `obj.area` |
| `drawMatrixPeakMeter` | Multi-channel meter | `obj.area`, `obj.peaks` |
| `drawScrollbar` | Scrollbar | `obj.area`, `obj.handle` |

For the complete list of all 60+ registerable functions, type `ScriptLookAndFeel` in the API Browser.

### Global vs Local Scope

A **local LAF** applies only to the components you explicitly attach it to:

```javascript
const var laf = Content.createLocalLookAndFeel();
laf.registerFunction("drawRotarySlider", function(g, obj) { ... });

myKnob1.setLocalLookAndFeel(laf);  // this knob uses the custom LAF
myKnob2.setLocalLookAndFeel(laf);  // this one too
myKnob3;                           // this one keeps the default appearance
```

A **global LAF** applies to all matching component types across the entire plugin:

```javascript
const var globalLaf = Engine.createGlobalScriptLookAndFeel();
globalLaf.registerFunction("drawRotarySlider", function(g, obj) { ... });
// All rotary sliders in the plugin now use this draw function
```

Use local LAFs when different sections need different styles — one style for synth knobs, another for FX knobs. Use a global LAF when the whole plugin shares a unified look. You can mix both: set a global LAF for the baseline, then override specific components with local LAFs.

### Organizing LAF at Scale

As your UI grows, LAF code can become substantial. Projects evolve through a natural progression:

**Small projects** — one or two `registerFunction` calls inline in the interface script. Simple and direct.

**Medium projects** — a dedicated `LookAndFeel.js` file with a namespace:

```javascript
// LookAndFeel.js
namespace LookAndFeel
{
    const var knob = Content.createLocalLookAndFeel();
    knob.registerFunction("drawRotarySlider", function(g, obj) { ... });

    const var button = Content.createLocalLookAndFeel();
    button.registerFunction("drawToggleButton", function(g, obj) { ... });
}
```

```javascript
// Interface.js
include("LookAndFeel.js");

myKnob.setLocalLookAndFeel(LookAndFeel.knob);
myButton.setLocalLookAndFeel(LookAndFeel.button);
```

**Large projects** — a dedicated `LookAndFeels/` directory with type-organized files (`SliderLAF.js`, `ButtonLAF.js`, etc.), each exporting a namespace. Complex instruments can end up with 20+ LAF objects covering dozens of draw functions.


## CSS Styling

HISE supports CSS stylesheets as an alternative to scripted LAF for components with **discrete visual states**. If your component's appearance is driven by hover, active, checked, or disabled states — rather than continuous data — CSS is often cleaner and faster to write.

### When to Use CSS

The decision comes down to what drives the component's visuals:

| Visual Driver | Approach | Why |
|---------------|----------|-----|
| Discrete states (hover, active, checked) | **CSS** | Pseudo-selectors map directly to states |
| Continuous data (knob angle, meter level) | **Scripted LAF** | Requires procedural geometry (arcs, paths) |
| Both states and data | **CombinedLaf** | CSS handles the states, script handles the geometry |

**Rotary knobs are the canonical example** of why CSS alone isn't enough: they require `Path.addArc()` with angles computed from the knob's normalized value. This is fundamentally procedural geometry that CSS cannot express. Buttons, combo boxes, and tab selectors — where the visual difference between states is color, opacity, or pseudo-element changes — are ideal CSS candidates.

### Inline Stylesheets

Apply CSS directly to a LAF object:

```javascript
const var laf = Content.createLocalLookAndFeel();

laf.setInlineStyleSheet("
    button {
        background-color: #333333;
        border-radius: 4px;
        color: white;
        transition: background-color 0.1s;
    }

    button:hover {
        background-color: #444444;
    }

    button:active {
        background-color: #3399FF;
    }

    button:checked {
        background-color: #3399FF;
    }
");

myButton.setLocalLookAndFeel(laf);
```

HISE maps component types to CSS element selectors: `button` for ScriptButton, `select` for ScriptComboBox, `input[type='range']` for ScriptSlider. Pseudo-classes (`:hover`, `:active`, `:checked`, `:focus`, `:disabled`) map to component interaction states.

### External CSS Files

For larger stylesheets, load from a `.css` file in your `Scripts/` folder:

```javascript
laf.setStyleSheet("main.css");
```

### CSS Variables

Pass dynamic values from script to CSS using `setStyleSheetProperty()`:

```javascript
// Set a CSS variable on the LAF
laf.setStyleSheetProperty("--accent-color", "#3399FF", "");

// Set a variable on a specific component
myButton.setStyleSheetProperty("--icon", "path-data-here", "");
```

Reference these in CSS with `var()`:

```css
button {
    background-color: var(--accent-color);
}
```

### CSS Classes

Assign CSS class selectors to individual components for targeted styling:

```javascript
myButton.setStyleSheetClass(".bypass");
anotherButton.setStyleSheetClass(".page-button");
```

```css
.bypass {
    background-color: #333;
}

.bypass:checked {
    background-color: #FF4444;
}

.page-button {
    border-bottom: 2px solid transparent;
}

.page-button:checked {
    border-bottom: 2px solid #3399FF;
}
```

### Pseudo-Elements

CSS `::before` and `::after` pseudo-elements work for adding decorative layers:

```css
button::before {
    content: '';
    background-color: var(--icon-color);
    /* mask or background-image for icon rendering */
}

button:checked::before {
    background-color: #3399FF;
}
```

### CombinedLaf

When a LAF object has both `registerFunction()` calls and CSS methods, HISE creates a **CombinedLaf** that dispatches per-function. For each draw callback, it checks: if a JavaScript function is registered, script executes; otherwise, CSS renders.

This lets you use CSS for buttons and combo boxes while keeping scripted drawing for knobs and visualizers — the best of both worlds:

```javascript
const var laf = Content.createLocalLookAndFeel();

// CSS for buttons and combo boxes (state-driven)
laf.setInlineStyleSheet("
    button { background: #333; color: white; border-radius: 4px; }
    button:hover { background: #444; }
    button:checked { background: #3399FF; }
    select { background: #333; color: white; }
");

// Script for rotary sliders (data-driven)
laf.registerFunction("drawRotarySlider", function(g, obj)
{
    // Arc drawing with obj.valueNormalized...
});

// One LAF serves both purposes
myKnob.setLocalLookAndFeel(laf);
myButton.setLocalLookAndFeel(laf);
myComboBox.setLocalLookAndFeel(laf);
```


## Filmstrip Images

For pixel-perfect, pre-rendered controls, HISE supports **filmstrip images** — vertical PNG strips where each frame represents a different control position. A knob with 128 frames has all 128 positions pre-rendered in a single tall image.

Set the filmstrip via the component's `filmstripImage` property, or load it in script:

```javascript
myKnob.set("filmstripImage", "{PROJECT_FOLDER}images/knob_strip.png");
myKnob.set("numStrips", 128);       // number of frames in the strip
myKnob.set("isVertical", true);     // strip orientation
```

Filmstrips offer the fastest rendering (simple image blit, no geometry) and pixel-perfect results, but they're inflexible — you can't change colors at runtime, you can't show modulation rings, and they scale poorly to different resolutions. Use them when visual fidelity matters more than dynamic behavior, or when porting a UI from another framework that uses pre-rendered assets.


## Visual Feedback

Real-time displays — peak meters, filter curves, modulation indicators — use **timer callbacks** to poll audio engine state and repaint continuously. The pattern is always the same: timer polls a value → stores it in `this.data` → calls `this.repaint()` → paint routine reads `this.data` and draws.

### Peak Meter

A stereo level meter polling the master output:

```javascript
const var Meter = Content.addPanel("Meter", 10, 10);
Meter.set("width", 200);
Meter.set("height", 20);

Meter.setPaintRoutine(function(g)
{
    var a = this.getLocalBounds(0);
    var h = a.getHeight() / 2;

    g.fillAll(0xFF222222);

    // Left channel
    g.setColour(0xFF3399FF);
    g.fillRect([0, 0, this.data.left * a.getWidth(), h - 1]);

    // Right channel
    g.fillRect([0, h + 1, this.data.right * a.getWidth(), h - 1]);
});

Meter.setTimerCallback(function()
{
    this.data.left = Engine.getMasterPeakLevel(0);
    this.data.right = Engine.getMasterPeakLevel(1);
    this.repaint();
});

Meter.startTimer(30); // ~33 fps
```

30ms is the standard timer interval — fast enough for smooth visual feedback without burning CPU. For meters that need peak-hold or decay, store the peak in `this.data` and decay it by a constant each timer tick.

### Modulation Indicator

A small LED showing a modulator's real-time output level:

```javascript
const var Led = Content.addPanel("ModLed", 10, 10);
Led.set("width", 12);
Led.set("height", 12);

// Get a reference to the modulator
const var lfo = Synth.getModulator("LFO1");

Led.setPaintRoutine(function(g)
{
    var level = this.data.level;

    // Interpolate from dark to bright based on modulator output
    var r = parseInt(level * 0x33);
    var gr = parseInt(level * 0xFF);
    var b = parseInt(level * 0x33);
    var col = (0xFF << 24) | (r << 16) | (gr << 8) | b;

    g.setColour(col);
    g.fillEllipse(this.getLocalBounds(0));
});

Led.setTimerCallback(function()
{
    this.data.level = lfo.getCurrentLevel();
    this.repaint();
});

Led.startTimer(30);
```

### Filter Display via LAF

For built-in visualizer widgets like filter graphs, you customize the display through LAF rather than ScriptPanel. The filter display FloatingTile provides its own draw functions:

```javascript
laf.registerFunction("drawFilterBackground", function(g, obj)
{
    g.fillAll(0xFF1A1A1A);

    // Draw grid lines
    g.setColour(0x20FFFFFF);
    for (var i = 0; i < 5; i++)
    {
        var y = obj.area.getHeight() * i / 5;
        g.drawHorizontalLine(y, 0, obj.area.getWidth());
    }
});

laf.registerFunction("drawFilterPath", function(g, obj)
{
    g.setColour(0xFF3399FF);
    g.fillPath(obj.path, obj.area);

    g.setColour(0xFFFFFFFF);
    g.drawPath(obj.path, obj.area, 2.0);
});
```

The engine provides `obj.path` — the pre-calculated frequency response path. You don't compute the filter response yourself; you just choose how to render it.

### Waveform Display via LAF

Similarly, audio thumbnail rendering is customized through LAF:

```javascript
laf.registerFunction("drawThumbnailPath", function(g, obj)
{
    g.setColour(0xFF3399FF);
    g.fillPath(obj.path, obj.area);
});

laf.registerFunction("drawThumbnailBackground", function(g, obj)
{
    g.fillAll(0xFF1A1A1A);
});

laf.registerFunction("drawThumbnailRuler", function(g, obj)
{
    g.setColour(0xFFFFFFFF);
    g.fillRect([obj.x, 0, 2, obj.area.getHeight()]);
});
```

The engine passes the waveform path, playback position, and range data — your LAF functions control only the visual presentation.


## Putting It All Together

A well-structured HISE UI typically uses all three systems in concert:

- **ScriptPanel paint routines** for truly custom controls (XY pads, sequencer grids, custom meters) and for layout containers (page switching, section backgrounds)
- **Look and Feel** for styling the built-in components (knobs, buttons, combo boxes) that make up the bulk of any plugin's controls
- **CSS** (optionally, via CombinedLaf) for state-driven components where pseudo-selectors and transitions produce cleaner code than scripted conditionals

The systems are complementary, not competing. A typical production interface might have 5–10 LAF objects covering 15–20 draw functions, a handful of ScriptPanels for custom displays and meters, and CSS for button/tab styling — all working together to create a unified visual identity.


## What's Next

- **Back to structure →** [The UI Model](/v2/architecture/ui-model) — component types, the Content API, and property system
- **Presets →** [The Preset System](/v2/guide/preset-system) — how `saveInPreset` and `isPluginParameter` connect your UI to the data model
- **Build one →** [Building a Sampler](/v2/guide/building-a-sampler) or [Building a Synth](/v2/guide/building-a-synth) — apply custom UI to a real project

text:
Building Custom Controls

Paint routines become genuinely useful when combined with **mouse callbacks** and **`this.data`** for state storage. Together, these three pieces let you build fully interactive controls.

### Mouse Callbacks

Panels respond to mouse input through `setMouseCallback()`:

```javascript
Panel1.setMouseCallback(function(event)
{
    if (event.clicked)
        Console.print("Clicked at " + event.x + ", " + event.y);

    if (event.hover)
        this.repaint();
});
```

Before a panel responds to mouse events, set its `allowCallbacks` property to control which events fire:

| Level | Events |
|-------|--------|
| `"No Callbacks"` | None (default) |
| `"Context Menu"` | Right-click popup only |
| `"Clicks Only"` | Click, double-click, mouse-up |
| `"Clicks & Hover"` | + hover enter/exit |
| `"Clicks, Hover & Dragging"` | + drag events |
| `"All Callbacks"` | + every mouse movement |

Key event properties:

| Property | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| `event.clicked` | True on mouse-down |
| `event.mouseUp` | True on mouse-up |
| `event.doubleClick` | True on double-click |
| `event.rightClick` | True if right button |
| `event.hover` | True when hovering (requires `"Clicks & Hover"+`) |
| `event.drag` | True during drag (requires `"Clicks, Hover & Dragging"+`) |
| `event.x`, `event.y` | Current mouse position |
| `event.dragX`, `event.dragY` | Distance dragged from start |
| `event.shiftDown`, `event.cmdDown`, `event.altDown` | Modifier keys |

### Example: A Custom Toggle Button

A minimal toggle that draws a filled circle when on, an outlined circle when off, and highlights on hover:

```javascript
const var Toggle = Content.addPanel("Toggle", 10, 10);
Toggle.set("width", 40);
Toggle.set("height", 40);
Toggle.set("allowCallbacks", "Clicks & Hover");
Toggle.set("saveInPreset", true);

Toggle.setPaintRoutine(function(g)
{
    var a = this.getLocalBounds(0).reduced(4);
    var on = this.getValue();
    var hov = this.data.hover;

    // Background
    g.setColour(hov ? 0xFF444444 : 0xFF333333);
    g.fillEllipse(a);

    if (on)
    {
        g.setColour(0xFF3399FF);
        g.fillEllipse(a.reduced(6));
    }
    else
    {
        g.setColour(0xFF888888);
        g.drawEllipse(a.reduced(6), 2.0);
    }
});

Toggle.setMouseCallback(function(event)
{
    if (event.clicked)
    {
        this.setValue(1 - this.getValue());
        this.changed();   // triggers control callback + preset save
    }

    this.data.hover = event.hover;
    this.repaint();
});
```

The key pattern: `this.setValue()` updates the stored value, and `this.changed()` tells the engine the value changed — triggering any attached control callback and marking the preset as dirty. Without `this.changed()`, the value updates visually but the engine doesn't know about it.

### Example: A Custom Slider

A horizontal slider using drag events. The drag distance maps to the value range:

```javascript
const var Slider1 = Content.addPanel("Slider1", 10, 60);
Slider1.set("width", 200);
Slider1.set("height", 30);
Slider1.set("allowCallbacks", "Clicks, Hover & Dragging");
Slider1.set("saveInPreset", true);

Slider1.setPaintRoutine(function(g)
{
    var a = this.getLocalBounds(0);
    var val = this.getValue();

    // Track background
    g.setColour(0xFF333333);
    g.fillRoundedRectangle(a.reduced(0, 8), 4);

    // Filled portion
    g.setColour(0xFF3399FF);
    var filled = a.reduced(0, 8);
    filled = filled.withWidth(filled.getWidth() * val);
    g.fillRoundedRectangle(filled, 4);

    // Value text
    g.setColour(0xFFFFFFFF);
    g.drawAlignedText(Math.round(val * 100) + "%", a, "centred");
});

Slider1.setMouseCallback(function(event)
{
    if (event.clicked)
    {
        // Store the starting value for drag reference
        this.data.startValue = this.getValue();
    }

    if (event.drag)
    {
        // Map horizontal drag to 0–1 range
        var delta = event.dragX / this.getWidth();
        var newVal = Math.range(this.data.startValue + delta, 0, 1);
        this.setValue(newVal);
        this.changed();
        this.repaint();
    }

    if (event.doubleClick)
    {
        this.setValue(0.5);   // reset to default
        this.changed();
        this.repaint();
    }
});
```

This demonstrates the drag pattern: capture the starting value on `event.clicked`, compute a delta on `event.drag`, and clamp the result. The `event.dragX` property gives cumulative horizontal distance from the click point.

### Using `this.data`

Every ScriptPanel has a `this.data` object — a free-form property bag for storing custom state. It persists across paint/mouse/timer callbacks but is **not saved in presets**. Use it for transient UI state: hover flags, animation progress, cached calculations, drag start positions.

```javascript
this.data.hover = event.hover;           // hover state for paint routine
this.data.startValue = this.getValue();  // drag reference
this.data.level = 0.0;                   // meter level from timer
this.data.animationPhase = 0.0;          // animation counter
```

For persisted state, use `this.getValue()` / `this.setValue()` — these participate in the preset and automation system.

