text:
Look and Feel

Look and Feel (LAF) lets you override how **built-in component types** draw without replacing them with ScriptPanels. The components retain all their built-in behavior — value handling, automation, preset saving, mouse interaction — you just replace the visuals.

### Creating and Attaching a LAF

```javascript
const var laf = Content.createLocalLookAndFeel();

laf.registerFunction("drawRotarySlider", function(g, obj)
{
    // Your custom drawing code here
});

// Attach to specific components
myKnob1.setLocalLookAndFeel(laf);
myKnob2.setLocalLookAndFeel(laf);
```

One LAF object can serve multiple components. Register draw functions for the component types you want to customize — everything else renders with the default appearance.

### The `obj` Parameter

Every LAF function receives `function(g, obj)` where `obj` contains the component's current state. The properties vary by function, but the core set is consistent:

**Common to all LAF functions:**

| Property | Type | Description |
|----------|------|-------------|
| `obj.area` | Rectangle | The component's drawable area |
| `obj.text` | String | The component's display text |
| `obj.enabled` | bool | Whether the component is enabled |
| `obj.bgColour` | int | Background color (from property) |
| `obj.itemColour1` | int | First accent color |
| `obj.itemColour2` | int | Second accent color |
| `obj.textColour` | int | Text color |

**`drawRotarySlider` adds:**

| Property | Type | Description |
|----------|------|-------------|
| `obj.value` | double | Current value (in the component's range) |
| `obj.valueNormalized` | double | Value mapped to 0.0–1.0 |
| `obj.valueSuffixString` | String | Formatted value with suffix (e.g. `"440.0 Hz"`) |
| `obj.suffix` | String | Just the suffix (e.g. `" Hz"`) |
| `obj.min`, `obj.max` | double | Value range |
| `obj.skew` | double | Skew factor |
| `obj.clicked` | bool | Mouse button is down |
| `obj.hover` | bool | Mouse is over the component |
| `obj.modulationActive` | bool | A modulation source is connected |
| `obj.valueFromModulation` | double | Modulated normalized value (0.0–1.0) |
| `obj.modMinValue`, `obj.modMaxValue` | double | Modulation range limits (normalized) |

**`drawToggleButton` adds:**

| Property | Type | Description |
|----------|------|-------------|
| `obj.value` | bool | Current toggle state |
| `obj.over` | bool | Mouse is hovering |
| `obj.down` | bool | Mouse button is down |

**`drawComboBox` adds:**

| Property | Type | Description |
|----------|------|-------------|
| `obj.text` | String | Currently selected item text |
| `obj.active` | bool | An item is selected |
| `obj.hover` | bool | Mouse is over or popup is open |
| `obj.down` | bool | Mouse button is down |

### Drawing a Rotary Slider

The most common LAF function. Rotary knobs need arc geometry — you compute start and end angles from the normalized value, then draw the arc:

```javascript
laf.registerFunction("drawRotarySlider", function(g, obj)
{
    var a = obj.area;
    var val = obj.valueNormalized;

    // Angle range: -135° to +135° (in radians)
    var startAngle = -2.356;   // -3π/4
    var endAngle = 2.356;      //  3π/4
    var currentAngle = startAngle + val * (endAngle - startAngle);

    // Background track
    g.setColour(0xFF333333);
    g.drawArc(a.reduced(6), startAngle, endAngle, 3.0);

    // Value arc
    g.setColour(0xFF3399FF);
    g.drawArc(a.reduced(6), startAngle, currentAngle, 3.0);

    // Modulation ring (when a modulator is connected)
    if (obj.modulationActive)
    {
        var modAngle = startAngle + obj.valueFromModulation * (endAngle - startAngle);
        g.setColour(0x5500FF88);
        g.drawArc(a.reduced(2), currentAngle, modAngle, 2.0);
    }

    // Value text
    g.setColour(obj.hover ? 0xFFFFFFFF : 0xFFAAAAAA);
    g.setFont("default", 12.0);
    g.drawAlignedText(obj.hover ? obj.valueSuffixString : obj.text,
                      a, "centred");
});
```

The modulation ring is a key detail for synth UIs — when a modulator is routed to a knob, `obj.modulationActive` becomes true and `obj.valueFromModulation` reflects the modulated position. Drawing a second arc between the base value and the modulated value gives the user visual feedback on modulation depth.

### Drawing a Toggle Button

Buttons are simpler — they have discrete states rather than continuous values:

```javascript
laf.registerFunction("drawToggleButton", function(g, obj)
{
    var a = obj.area;

    // Background
    g.setColour(obj.value ? 0xFF3399FF : 0xFF333333);
    g.fillRoundedRectangle(a, 4.0);

    // Hover highlight
    if (obj.over)
    {
        g.setColour(0x15FFFFFF);
        g.fillRoundedRectangle(a, 4.0);
    }

    // Label
    g.setColour(obj.value ? 0xFFFFFFFF : 0xFF999999);
    g.setFont("default", 13.0);
    g.drawAlignedText(obj.text, a, "centred");
});
```

`drawToggleButton` is the most frequently customized LAF function — buttons vary the most across plugin designs (icon buttons, pill toggles, tab selectors, solo/mute buttons).

### Drawing a Combo Box

When you register `drawComboBox`, you take full control of the dropdown's closed-state appearance. HISE hides the default text label so you can draw everything yourself:

```javascript
laf.registerFunction("drawComboBox", function(g, obj)
{
    var a = obj.area;

    // Background
    g.setColour(obj.hover ? 0xFF444444 : 0xFF333333);
    g.fillRoundedRectangle(a, 4.0);

    // Selected item text
    g.setColour(0xFFFFFFFF);
    g.setFont("default", 14.0);
    g.drawAlignedText(obj.text, [a[0] + 10, a[1], a[2] - 30, a[3]], "left");

    // Dropdown arrow
    var arrow = Content.createPath();
    arrow.startNewSubPath(0.0, 0.0);
    arrow.lineTo(0.5, 1.0);
    arrow.lineTo(1.0, 0.0);

    g.setColour(0xFFAAAAAA);
    g.fillPath(arrow, [a[0] + a[2] - 22, a[1] + a[3] * 0.5 - 3, 12, 6]);
});
```

### Other Key Draw Functions

Beyond the three core types, LAF covers the entire HISE widget set. Some commonly used functions:

| Function | Component | Key `obj` Properties |
|----------|-----------|---------------------|
| `drawLinearSlider` | Horizontal/vertical sliders | Same as rotary, plus `obj.style` |
| `drawPopupMenuBackground` | Popup menu container | `obj.area` |
| `drawPopupMenuItem` | Popup menu item | `obj.text`, `obj.ticked`, `obj.highlighted`, `obj.isSeparator`, `obj.isSectionHeader` |
| `drawPresetBrowserBackground` | Preset browser | `obj.area` |
| `drawPresetBrowserListItem` | Preset browser item | `obj.text`, `obj.selected`, `obj.area` |
| `drawAhdsrPath` | AHDSR envelope display | `obj.path` (the envelope path), `obj.area` |
| `drawFilterBackground` | Filter frequency display | `obj.area` |
| `drawFilterPath` | Filter response curve | `obj.path`, `obj.area`, `obj.filterIndex` |
| `drawWhiteNote` / `drawBlackNote` | MIDI keyboard | `obj.keyWidth`, `obj.noteNumber`, `obj.on` |
| `drawTablePath` | Table editor curve | `obj.path`, `obj.area` |
| `drawMatrixPeakMeter` | Multi-channel meter | `obj.area`, `obj.peaks` |
| `drawScrollbar` | Scrollbar | `obj.area`, `obj.handle` |

For the complete list of all 60+ registerable functions, type `ScriptLookAndFeel` in the API Browser.

### Global vs Local Scope

A **local LAF** applies only to the components you explicitly attach it to:

```javascript
const var laf = Content.createLocalLookAndFeel();
laf.registerFunction("drawRotarySlider", function(g, obj) { ... });

myKnob1.setLocalLookAndFeel(laf);  // this knob uses the custom LAF
myKnob2.setLocalLookAndFeel(laf);  // this one too
myKnob3;                           // this one keeps the default appearance
```

