CSS Styling

HISE supports CSS stylesheets as an alternative to scripted LAF for components with **discrete visual states**. If your component's appearance is driven by hover, active, checked, or disabled states — rather than continuous data — CSS is often cleaner and faster to write.

### When to Use CSS

The decision comes down to what drives the component's visuals:

| Visual Driver | Approach | Why |
|---------------|----------|-----|
| Discrete states (hover, active, checked) | **CSS** | Pseudo-selectors map directly to states |
| Continuous data (knob angle, meter level) | **Scripted LAF** | Requires procedural geometry (arcs, paths) |
| Both states and data | **CombinedLaf** | CSS handles the states, script handles the geometry |

**Rotary knobs are the canonical example** of why CSS alone isn't enough: they require `Path.addArc()` with angles computed from the knob's normalized value. This is fundamentally procedural geometry that CSS cannot express. Buttons, combo boxes, and tab selectors — where the visual difference between states is color, opacity, or pseudo-element changes — are ideal CSS candidates.

### Inline Stylesheets

Apply CSS directly to a LAF object:

```javascript
const var laf = Content.createLocalLookAndFeel();

laf.setInlineStyleSheet("
    button {
        background-color: #333333;
        border-radius: 4px;
        color: white;
        transition: background-color 0.1s;
    }

    button:hover {
        background-color: #444444;
    }

    button:active {
        background-color: #3399FF;
    }

    button:checked {
        background-color: #3399FF;
    }
");

myButton.setLocalLookAndFeel(laf);
```

HISE maps component types to CSS element selectors: `button` for ScriptButton, `select` for ScriptComboBox, `input[type='range']` for ScriptSlider. Pseudo-classes (`:hover`, `:active`, `:checked`, `:focus`, `:disabled`) map to component interaction states.

### External CSS Files

For larger stylesheets, load from a `.css` file in your `Scripts/` folder:

```javascript
laf.setStyleSheet("main.css");
```

### CSS Variables

Pass dynamic values from script to CSS using `setStyleSheetProperty()`:

```javascript
// Set a CSS variable on the LAF
laf.setStyleSheetProperty("--accent-color", "#3399FF", "");

// Set a variable on a specific component
myButton.setStyleSheetProperty("--icon", "path-data-here", "");
```

Reference these in CSS with `var()`:

```css
button {
    background-color: var(--accent-color);
}
```

### CSS Classes

Assign CSS class selectors to individual components for targeted styling:

```javascript
myButton.setStyleSheetClass(".bypass");
anotherButton.setStyleSheetClass(".page-button");
```

```css
.bypass {
    background-color: #333;
}

.bypass:checked {
    background-color: #FF4444;
}

.page-button {
    border-bottom: 2px solid transparent;
}

.page-button:checked {
    border-bottom: 2px solid #3399FF;
}
```

### Pseudo-Elements

CSS `after` pseudo-elements work for adding decorative layers:

```css
buttonbefore {
    background-color: #3399FF;
}
```

### CombinedLaf

When a LAF object has both `registerFunction()` calls and CSS methods, HISE creates a **CombinedLaf** that dispatches per-function. For each draw callback, it checks: if a JavaScript function is registered, script executes; otherwise, CSS renders.

This lets you use CSS for buttons and combo boxes while keeping scripted drawing for knobs and visualizers — the best of both worlds:

```javascript
const var laf = Content.createLocalLookAndFeel();

// CSS for buttons and combo boxes (state-driven)
laf.setInlineStyleSheet("
    button { background: #333; color: white; border-radius: 4px; }
    button:hover { background: #444; }
    button:checked { background: #3399FF; }
    select { background: #333; color: white; }
");

// Script for rotary sliders (data-driven)
laf.registerFunction("drawRotarySlider", function(g, obj)
{
    // Arc drawing with obj.valueNormalized...
});

// One LAF serves both purposes
myKnob.setLocalLookAndFeel(laf);
myButton.setLocalLookAndFeel(laf);
myComboBox.setLocalLookAndFeel(laf);
```

