Similarly, audio thumbnail rendering is customized through LAF:

```javascript
laf.registerFunction("drawThumbnailPath", function(g, obj)
{
    g.setColour(0xFF3399FF);
    g.fillPath(obj.path, obj.area);
});

laf.registerFunction("drawThumbnailBackground", function(g, obj)
{
    g.fillAll(0xFF1A1A1A);
});

laf.registerFunction("drawThumbnailRuler", function(g, obj)
{
    g.setColour(0xFFFFFFFF);
    g.fillRect([obj.x, 0, 2, obj.area.getHeight()]);
});
```

The engine passes the waveform path, playback position, and range data — your LAF functions control only the visual presentation.

This guide covers the practical side of HISE's event and messaging system — when to use each connection tier, how to wire Broadcasters, and the patterns that scale. It assumes you've read [Data Flow & Messaging](/v2/architecture/data-flow-messaging), which explains the three connection tiers and why they exist. This page is about putting them to work.


## Choosing Your Connection Tier

The [escalation path](/v2/architecture/data-flow-messaging#the-connection-tiers) gives you three options. The decision isn't "which is best" — it's "which fits this specific connection."

| Question | `processorId` | `setControlCallback` | Broadcaster |
|----------|:---:|:---:|:---:|
| One knob → one parameter, no logic? | **✅ Use this** | Works but overkill | Overkill |
| Need math, scaling, or conditions? | Can't | **✅ Use this** | Overkill |
| One knob → multiple parameters? | Can't | **✅ Use this** | Either works |
| One event → 3+ independent targets? | Can't | Gets messy | **✅ Use this** |
| Need reverse notification from module? | Built-in | Manual polling | **✅ Use this** |
| Need to debug the data flow visually? | N/A | No visibility | **✅ Broadcaster Map** |

### The Three Rules

**Rule 1: Start with `processorId`.** If a knob maps to one parameter with no transformation, set `processorId` and `parameterId` in the property panel and move on. Zero code, bidirectional, preset-safe. This should be your default for every simple connection.

**Rule 2: Upgrade to `setControlCallback` when you need logic.** The moment you need to scale a value, control two parameters from one knob, or add a condition — write an inline function callback. One callback per component, full scripting power.

**Rule 3: Upgrade to Broadcaster when you need decoupling.** The moment one event needs to reach multiple independent targets, or you need to react to module parameter changes from any source (user, DAW, preset), or you're coordinating state across UI pages — use a Broadcaster.

Most projects settle into a natural distribution: ~70% `processorId`, ~20% `setControlCallback`, ~10% Broadcaster. The percentages shift as project complexity grows — a simple effect plugin might use zero Broadcasters, while a multi-page synth with preset management might have 10–15.

**The hybrid approach is intentional.** Don't feel pressure to standardize on one tier. A project with 30 knobs might use `processorId` for 22, `setControlCallback` for 6 where scaling or multi-target logic is needed, and 2 Broadcasters for page state and preset notifications. Each connection uses the simplest tier that handles the job. If you later need to add targets, upgrading a `setControlCallback` to a Broadcaster is straightforward — the source and target wiring replaces the callback body.

<!-- SVG: events-messaging-decision-flow.svg — A simple decision flowchart. Start: "New connection needed". First diamond: "Direct 1:1, no logic?" → Yes → "processorId" (done). No → Second diamond: "Need logic, 1-2 targets?" → Yes → "setControlCallback" (done). No → "Broadcaster" (done). Use HISE green (#78be8a) for the decision arrows, dark panels (#353535) for the boxes, light text (#E0E0E0). Clean, generous spacing. -->


## When Callbacks Hit Their Limit

With `setControlCallback`, every target lives inside one function body. This works until it doesn't:

```javascript
// This callback started simple. Then requirements grew.
inline function onMasterGainControl(component, value)
{
    masterGain.setAttribute(masterGain.Gain, value);     // original purpose
    lblGain.set("text", Math.round(value) + " dB");      // added: label update
    meter.set("visible", value > -60);                    // added: meter visibility
    headerPanel.repaint();                                // added: header redraw
    if (value < -59) muteButton.setValue(1);              // added: auto-mute
};
```

Five concerns in one function. Adding a sixth means editing this callback again. Removing the meter means editing it again. Testing the label update in isolation is impossible — every change runs the whole block. The callback has become a monolith.

A Broadcaster solves this by giving each concern its own target. Add, remove, or test any target independently.


## Working with Broadcasters

### Creating a Broadcaster

Every Broadcaster needs an `id` and an argument list that defines its message signature:

```javascript
const var gainBC = Engine.createBroadcaster({
    id: "masterGain",
    args: ["component", "value"]
});
```

The `id` appears in the Broadcaster Map and error messages — make it descriptive. The `args` array names the values that flow through the Broadcaster. Sources and targets must match this argument count.

::Tip{title="IDE shortcut"}
HISE includes a **Broadcaster Wizard** that generates source and target wiring code. It scaffolds common patterns without writing boilerplate. [VERIFY: What is the access path — right-click menu? Toolbar button?]
::

### Attaching Sources

Sources tell the Broadcaster **what to watch**. Each `attachTo*` method watches a different event type.

**Component values** — the most common source. Fires when a knob, button, or combo box value changes:

```javascript
// Watch a single component
gainBC.attachToComponentValue("knobGain", "");

// Watch multiple components (same Broadcaster, same signature)
gainBC.attachToComponentValue(["knobGain", "knobTrim"], "");
```

The second argument is metadata — a string or object for identification in the Broadcaster Map. Use `""` for quick prototyping, but use descriptive strings in real projects — `"gainKnob"` or `"filterControls"`. For complex projects, pass a full metadata object:

```javascript
gainBC.attachToComponentValue("knobGain", {
    id: "gainSource",
    comment: "Watches the master gain knob",
    tags: ["mixer"]
});
```

This metadata appears in the Broadcaster Map and makes debugging significantly easier.

**Module parameters** — watches processor parameters from any source (user, DAW automation, preset load, script). This is how you get **reverse notification** — something `setControlCallback` can't do:

```javascript
const var paramBC = Engine.createBroadcaster({
    id: "filterWatch",
    args: ["processorId", "parameterId", "value"]
});

// Watch two parameters on one processor
paramBC.attachToModuleParameter("PolyphonicFilter", ["Frequency", "Q"], "");
```

**Radio groups** — fires when any button in a numbered radio group is selected. The argument is the index of the selected button:

```javascript
const var pageBC = Engine.createBroadcaster({
    id: "pageSelector",
    args: ["index"]
});

pageBC.attachToRadioGroup(1, "");  // radio group number 1
```

Other source types — `attachToComponentMouseEvents` for drag interactions, `attachToOtherBroadcaster` for chaining, `attachToComplexData` for AudioFile/SliderPack changes — follow the same pattern. See [Data Flow & Messaging — Source Types](/v2/architecture/data-flow-messaging#source-types) for the full category list.

### Adding Targets

Targets tell the Broadcaster **what to do** when it fires.

**General listener** — full scripting flexibility. The callback receives the Broadcaster's arguments:

```javascript
gainBC.addListener(0, "applyGain",
    function(component, value)
    {
        masterGain.setAttribute(masterGain.Gain, value);
    });
```

The first argument is the `this` object for the callback (use `0` or `null` when you don't need one). The second is metadata — a string identifying this listener in the Broadcaster Map.

**Component property listener** — sets component properties declaratively. The optional transform function converts the Broadcaster's value into the property value:

```javascript
gainBC.addComponentPropertyListener(["lblGain"], ["text"], "gainLabel",
    function(index, component, property, value)
    {
        return Math.round(value) + " dB";
    });
```

Without the transform function, the Broadcaster's value is assigned directly to the property. With it, you return the transformed value. This is the most common target type for UI synchronization — labels, colors, visibility, tooltips.

**Component refresh listener** — triggers a visual update without changing values. Useful for panels with custom paint routines:

```javascript
gainBC.addComponentRefreshListener(["headerPanel"], "repaint", "headerRedraw");
```

Refresh types: `"repaint"`, `"changed"`, `"updateValueFromProcessorConnection"`, `"loseFocus"`, `"resetValueToDefault"`.

**Delayed listener** — fires after a specified delay. Useful for debouncing or sequencing:

```javascript
gainBC.addDelayedListener(200, 0, "debouncedSave",
    function(component, value)
    {
        Engine.saveUserPreset("current");
    });
```

A new event resets the delay timer — so rapid knob turns only trigger the save once, 200ms after the last change.


## Patterns You'll Use

### Multi-Page Navigation

The most common Broadcaster pattern. Buttons in a radio group switch visible UI pages:

```javascript
const var pageBC = Engine.createBroadcaster({
    id: "pageNav",
    args: ["index"]
});

pageBC.attachToRadioGroup(1, "");

// Build the panel list from component names
const var pages = [];
for (i = 0; i < 4; i++)
    pages[i] = Content.getComponent("page" + i);

// One target handles all page visibility
pageBC.addListener(0, "showPage",
    function(index)
    {
        for (i = 0; i < pages.length; i++)
            pages[i].set("visible", i == index);
    });
```

Adding a fifth page means adding a component and extending the loop — no Broadcaster changes. Adding a second target (e.g., updating a page title label) is one `addComponentPropertyListener` call.

### Bidirectional Parameter Display

Show a module parameter's current value in the UI, regardless of what changed it — user, DAW automation, or preset load:

```javascript
const var filterBC = Engine.createBroadcaster({
    id: "filterDisplay",
    args: ["processorId", "parameterId", "value"]
});

filterBC.attachToModuleParameter("PolyphonicFilter", "Frequency", "");

filterBC.addComponentPropertyListener(["lblFrequency"], ["text"], "freqLabel",
    function(index, component, property, value)
    {
        return Math.round(value) + " Hz";
    });
```

This solves a problem `setControlCallback` can't: when the DAW automation moves the filter frequency, the label updates automatically because `attachToModuleParameter` catches changes from any source.

### Preset-Aware UI Updates

When a preset loads, components with `saveInPreset` are restored automatically — but side effects (switching sample maps, rebuilding panel content, resetting visual state) need manual handling. A Broadcaster chain keeps this organized:

```javascript
const var presetBC = Engine.createBroadcaster({
    id: "presetLoaded",
    args: ["presetName"]
});

// Fire after preset load completes
presetBC.addListener(0, "refreshSamplers",
    function(presetName)
    {
        // Reload sample maps based on new preset state
        sampler.loadSampleMap(getCurrentMap());
    });

presetBC.addDelayedListener(100, 0, "refreshUI",
    function(presetName)
    {
        // Rebuild dynamic UI elements after a short delay
        rebuildPanels();
    });
```

The delayed listener ensures the UI refresh happens after all component values have settled. Multiple independent targets keep the concerns separated — sampler logic doesn't know about UI rebuilds.

For advanced Broadcaster patterns — EQ parameter coordination, drag-bypass interaction, centralized state bus architecture, and cross-Broadcaster event chains — see [Broadcaster Patterns](/v2/examples/broadcaster-patterns).

<!-- SVG: events-messaging-callback-vs-broadcaster.svg — Side-by-side comparison. Left: "Callback approach" — one box labeled "onMasterGainControl" with 5 arrows leaving to different targets (all tightly coupled). Right: "Broadcaster approach" — center hub labeled "masterGain BC" with one source arrow entering and 5 independent target arrows leaving. The left diagram looks tangled; the right looks clean. Use dark panels (#353535), HISE green (#78be8a) for Broadcaster arrows, #909090 for callback arrows. -->


## Debugging with the Broadcaster Map

When a UI element doesn't update or a parameter change doesn't propagate, the **Broadcaster Map** is the fastest diagnostic tool. It renders every Broadcaster in your project as a visual node graph — sources on the left, the Broadcaster hub in the center, targets on the right.

[VERIFY: How is the Broadcaster Map opened — menu path, keyboard shortcut, or toolbar button?]

To trace a problem:

1. **Find the Broadcaster** that should be carrying the event. Each node shows its `id` — this is why descriptive IDs matter.
2. **Check the source side.** Is the expected source attached? If you're watching a component value but the component ID is misspelled, the source won't appear.
3. **Check the target side.** Is the expected listener registered? Missing metadata makes unnamed targets hard to identify.
4. **Check the argument count.** Sources and targets must agree on the number of arguments. A component-value source provides 2 args (`component`, `value`); if a target expects 3, it won't fire.

The map also reveals architectural patterns you might not have planned — Broadcasters with only one target (consider simplifying to `setControlCallback`), Broadcasters with no sources (dead code), and unexpected chains where one Broadcaster triggers another.

For projects with 5+ Broadcasters, the map is essential. Without it, tracing event flow means grepping through script files. With it, the entire data flow topology is one panel.

<!-- SCREENSHOT: The Broadcaster Map showing a project with 3-4 Broadcasters, sources, and targets connected as a node graph -->


## Common Mistakes

**Over-engineering simple connections.** Don't use a Broadcaster for a knob that maps to one parameter. `processorId` does this with zero code. If you only have one target, `setControlCallback` is simpler and more readable. Broadcasters earn their weight at 3+ targets or when you need reverse notification.

**Skipping metadata.** Every `addListener` and `attachTo*` call takes a metadata argument. Passing `""` everywhere makes the Broadcaster Map useless — you'll see unnamed nodes with no way to identify what they do. Use descriptive strings: `"freqLabel"`, `"applyFilter"`, `"pageVisibility"`. In complex projects, use the full metadata object with `comment` and `tags`.

**Sync vs. async surprises.** By default, Broadcaster messages from sources are asynchronous — they're batched and delivered on the next message loop cycle. If your target needs to read a component value that was *just* set by the source, use `setForceSynchronousExecution(true)` on the Broadcaster. But don't make everything synchronous — async delivery is more efficient for UI updates.

**Forgetting `resendLastMessage`.** When you attach a target after the Broadcaster has already fired (common during initialization), the target won't receive the current state until the next source event. Call `bc.resendLastMessage(true)` after adding late targets to push the current state immediately.

**Not bypassing during batch operations.** When loading presets or performing bulk parameter updates, Broadcasters fire for every individual change — potentially hundreds of redundant updates. Use `bc.setBypassed(true)` before the batch, then `bc.setBypassed(false, true, false)` after — the second argument sends the final state to all targets in one shot.


## What's Next

- **Patterns →** [Broadcaster Patterns](/v2/examples/broadcaster-patterns) — six named patterns from production projects: EQ VCA, Drag Bypass, State Bus, Preset Chain, and more
- **Apply it →** [Building a Sampler](/v2/guide/building-a-sampler) — uses `setControlCallback` and `processorId` in a complete build
- **Related →** [Custom UI Design](/v2/guide/custom-ui) — ScriptPanel paint routines and Look and Feel, where Broadcasters drive visual updates
- **Related →** [Scripting In Depth](/v2/guide/scripting-in-depth) — arrays, shared callbacks, and the modular code pattern that pairs with Broadcasters
- **Back to →** [Data Flow & Messaging](/v2/architecture/data-flow-messaging) — the architectural model this guide builds on

