::method-heading
---
name: mix
params:
  - { name: colour1, type: Colour, desc: "The start colour (returned when alpha = 0.0)" }
  - { name: colour2, type: Colour, desc: "The end colour (returned when alpha = 1.0)" }
  - { name: alpha, type: double, desc: "Blend factor between the two colours" }
---
::

```javascript
var blended = Colours.mix(Colours.blue, Colours.green, 0.5);
```


Linearly interpolates between two colours in ARGB space. The blend factor controls the mix: 0.0 returns the first colour, 1.0 returns the second, and values between produce a proportional blend across all four channels. This is the standard way to create hover highlights in LAF paint callbacks - multiply `obj.hover` by a constant blend factor (e.g., 0.25) to avoid branching.

::warning{title="Blend factor not clamped to 0-1"}
The blend factor is not clamped to 0.0-1.0. Values outside this range produce extrapolated channel values that may wrap due to byte overflow, yielding unexpected colours.
::

#### Hover Highlight

```javascript
// Context: The idiomatic hover pattern used throughout professional HISE
// plugins. Multiplying a 0/1 hover flag by a constant blend factor
// avoids branching and keeps highlight intensity consistent across
// all controls.

const var HOVER_ALPHA = 0.25;

const var laf = Content.createLocalLookAndFeel();

laf.registerFunction("drawRotarySlider", function(g, obj)
{
    // obj.hover is 0.0 or 1.0 -- multiplying by HOVER_ALPHA produces
    // 0.0 (no change) or 0.25 (subtle white blend)
    var c = Colours.mix(obj.itemColour1, Colours.white, obj.hover * HOVER_ALPHA);
    g.setColour(c);
    g.fillEllipse(Rect.reduced(obj.area, 4.0));
});
```

#### Value Colour Transition

```javascript
// Context: Using a normalized 0.0-1.0 value as the blend factor to
// smoothly transition between an inactive and active colour.

const var panel = Content.addPanel("ModIndicator", 10, 10);
panel.set("width", 20);
panel.set("height", 20);

panel.setPaintRoutine(function(g)
{
    // Blend from dark grey to bright yellow based on modulation depth
    var c = Colours.mix(0xFF404040, 0xFFFFF600, this.data.value);
    g.setColour(c);
    g.fillEllipse(this.getLocalBounds(2));
});
```

